An idempotent job gives the same result whether it runs once or twice. A rerun should not double the rows. I delete the rows for the dates I am loading, then insert them again, inside one transaction. If the job fails halfway, the transaction rolls back and the old rows stay. In SQLite, an `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT(sales_date) DO UPDATE` is another option when the date is the primary key.

```sql
BEGIN;
DELETE FROM daily_revenue WHERE sales_date = '2024-12-31';
INSERT INTO daily_revenue (sales_date, revenue)
SELECT DATE(order_date), SUM(total_amount)
FROM orders
WHERE order_status = 'Delivered' AND order_date = '2024-12-31'
GROUP BY DATE(order_date);
COMMIT;
```
